<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gorizia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Görz, Gorica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gorizia is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soca">Isonzo</a> river in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. From around 1100 it was the seat of the counts of Gorizia, who built up lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, and in 1500 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it went to Italy, and in 1947 the new frontier left its eastern hinterland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, where the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-gorica">Nova Gorica</a> was built.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gorizia.jpg" alt="Gorizia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Viator slovenicus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gorizia-Gorica.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
